The mount is correct and the file is readable. Measured in the cluster: the ui2-db secret carries all four keys; the deployment sets UI2_DB_MIGRATE_USER_FILE=/run/secrets/ui2-db/migrate-user; the volume is mounted at that path; and a throwaway pod running as the same UID read the file and printed the user name.

So the path the service opens is not the path the deployment supplies.

PR #374 changed the runner's lifecycle, at
ui2/service/src/main/java/.../boot/MigrationStartupRunner.java:36:

  public class MigrationStartupRunner implements BeanFactoryPostProcessor, PriorityOrdered {
      ...
      @Override
      public int getOrder() { return Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE; }

and it is still constructed by an @Bean method taking injected values, at
ui2/service/src/main/java/.../boot/Ui2Application.java:76:

      @Value("${ui2.db.url}") String jdbcUrl,
      @Value("${ui2.db.migrate-user-file}") String userFile,
      @Value("${ui2.db.migrate-password-file}") String passwordFile,
      @Value("${ui2.db.migration-location:classpath:db/migration}") String migrationLocation) {
    return new MigrationStartupRunner(jdbcUrl, userFile, passwordFile, migrationLocation);

A bean that is itself a BeanFactoryPostProcessor is instantiated before the placeholder configurer -- which is a BeanFactoryPostProcessor too -- has run, so @Value on its factory method is not reliably resolved. Path.of() then receives something that is not the configured path, and SecretFile reports the resulting IOException as unreadable.

The unit tests did not catch it because they construct the runner with real values. Only a real start, against real configuration, exercises the resolution.
